Require LAN-scoped authorization on media streaming endpoints

Only DefaultPolicy is configured in ApiServiceCollectionExtensions -- there is no
FallbackPolicy and no global AuthorizeFilter -- so an action without an explicit
[Authorize] carries no authorization metadata and is served anonymously.

The item id is not a secret either: LibraryManager derives it as
MD5(type.FullName + path), so a guessable path yields a downloadable stream.

Verified against this server: GET /Items/{id} returns 401 while
GET /Audio/{id}/stream?static=true returns 206 with the media body, no token.

Why the built-in AnonymousLanAccessPolicy and not a bare [Authorize]:

An earlier revision of this patch used [Authorize]. That broke direct play. Upstream
leaves these routes anonymous ON PURPOSE -- HlsSegmentController even says so in a
comment ("requests come from Chrome without full query string") -- because streaming
clients cannot always attach credentials. Measured on 2026-09-02: Jellyfin Android TV
0.19.10 requests

    GET /Videos/{id}/stream?container=mkv&static=true&...

with no api_key parameter and no auth header. Under [Authorize] that 401s, the client
reports "Player error encountered, will retry", re-POSTs PlaybackInfo and falls back to
/videos/{id}/hls1/main/*.ts -- so playback silently drops off direct play onto a
server-side remux on every single start. The same URL returns 206 when api_key or
X-Emby-Token is supplied, so the endpoint is fine; the client simply sends neither.

Policies.AnonymousLanAccessPolicy (already registered upstream in
ApiServiceCollectionExtensions, handler AnonymousLanAccessHandler) permits anonymous
requests whose remote IP is in NetworkConfiguration.LocalNetworkSubnets and 401s the
rest. On this server that list is 192.168.1.0/24, 172.19.0.0/16 and 100.64.0.0/10, so
LAN clients, the Docker bridge and Tailscale peers all keep direct play, while any
genuinely off-network origin is refused.

Scope, stated honestly: every path that can currently reach this server is inside
LocalNetworkSubnets (Jellyfin is not in `tailscale serve` and is not funnelled), so
today this is equivalent in practice to stock behaviour. Its value is defence in depth
-- if Jellyfin is ever put behind a reverse proxy, funnel or port-forward, anonymous
streaming is already refused rather than needing to be remembered at that moment.

